+** OvVec: classify three successive overflow pages of a chain.
+**
+** Returns +1 if p0,p1,p2 are physically consecutive and ascending, -1 if they
+** are physically consecutive and descending, and 0 otherwise.
+**
+** A descending run is exactly as batchable as an ascending one -- the pages
+** still form a single contiguous extent on disk, they are merely consumed
+** from the high end.  This matters because SQLite's free-list returns pages
+** in descending order after a bulk DELETE, so in any database that has had a
+** large delete applied to it the overflow chains run backwards; a
+** forward-only test sees no contiguity at all in that (very common) case.
+*/
+static int ovvecRunDir(Pgno p0, Pgno p1, Pgno p2){
+  if( p0+1==p1 && p1+1==p2 ) return 1;
+  if( p1+1==p0 && p2+1==p1 ) return -1;
+  return 0;
+}
